# CLAUDE.md

> Claude reads **all** of this at the start of every session. Keep it **under a page**: what a new
> joiner needs on day one — commands, the conventions that matter, and the things Claude keeps
> getting wrong. Anything stale is context spent for nothing. Details live in the linked files.

## Mandatory reading order (binding — follow before answering ANY codebase question)

**First, classify the question.** *"What SHOULD it do"* (required behaviour, validation, rules) is
a **specification** question → start at step 2 and stop there. *"How DOES it work"* (call chain,
where a thing lives) is a **code** question → skip step 2 and start at step 3. Answering a
specification question from the code tells you what was built, never what was required — that is
how a confidently wrong answer gets produced.

Resolve [PROJECT_NAME] context in this order. Stop when answerable — don't proceed further.

1. **CLAUDE.md** — already loaded; check Permanent knowledge and Workflows sections first
2. **`.claude/planning/spec/`** — the governing requirement spec (`SPEC-NNN`). **Mandatory for any specification question.**
3. **`docs/feature-index.yaml`** — which files, aggregates, contracts, and ADRs own the feature being asked about
4. **`docs/glossary.md`** — look up domain terms before assuming meaning
5. **`.claude/planning/architecture/`** — how the mechanism works; layer map for legal dependencies
6. **Targeted `rg`** — search by symbol, per [`.claude/rules/code-navigation.md`](.claude/rules/code-navigation.md)
7. **Raw source files** — read only if steps 1–6 can't answer; read the minimum section (one method or constructor block, not the whole file)

**Never read raw source when an index can answer.** Indexes first = fewer tokens, faster answers,
and a citable trail. If an index turns out to be wrong, fix it before moving on — a stale index is
worse than none.

**Never cite a raw `.docx`/`.xlsx`/`.html` downstream** — cite `SPEC-NNN §R<n>`.
See [requirement-sources.md](.claude/rules/requirement-sources.md).

